# Interview Transcript with Rakha

**Q: Hello, can you tell me what your name is?**  
A: Rakha  

---

**Q: Who do you usually turn to for advice about course selection and why?**  
A: Mainly friends and seniors who have taken the course before, because they know a lot about it and have experience with it.  

---

**Q: Would your confidence change if the advice came from someone with the same degree background? Why or why not?**  
A: Probably from the same degree, but not necessarily a dual degree. I don’t think it makes a big difference.  

---

**Q: How confident would you be, on a scale of 1 to 10, if the advice came from someone with the same degree background?**  
A: Ten.  

---

**Q: Do you think it would be helpful to your studies?**  
A: Yeah, because I want to know what the course is about before enrolling.  

---

**Q: Do you think tailoring course options to your preferences, like avoiding finals or group work, would help you make course selections?**  
A: Yes, absolutely. I don’t like exams, and I usually take courses that have a lot of assignments but no exams. So I think it would be helpful.  

---

**Q: How much easier do you think it would be, on a scale of 1 to 10?**  
A: Ten.  

---

**Q: Tell me about a time when a course you wanted was full. How did it affect you?**  
A: I haven’t experienced that. Most of the courses I wanted to take weren’t full when I enrolled, so I haven’t had that issue.  

---

**Q: How do you usually figure out alternatives when enrolment doesn’t go as planned?**  
A: If the content of a course wasn’t what I expected, I would just drop it and choose another course.  

---

**Q: If you had access to automatic alternatives, like summer courses or path changes, when encountering such situations, would you use it?**  
A: Yeah.  

---

**Q: If so, on a scale of 1 to 10, how helpful do you think it would be in keeping students like you on track?**  
A: It would be helpful, but not a ten, would be an eight.  

---

**Q: Have you ever considered changing your major or minor? If so, what kind of questions or concerns came up during that process?**  
A: I thought about it last semester. I considered changing to “no major” because one course I took was really hard, and I wasn’t sure if I could survive in this major. But in the end, I didn’t change.  

---

**Q: Would you be open to changing your course plan, even if you don’t like what you’re studying?**  
A: Yeah.  

---

**Q: If you could safely explore different study paths without affecting your plan, would you use it? How do you imagine you would use it?**  
A: You mean, like, I could see what my study plan would look like if I changed my major? Yeah, I’d say that’s pretty helpful. If I could see what courses I would have to take for example, if I changed to machine learning. I think it would be really helpful without having to contact the faculty.  

---

**Q: Last question: on a scale of 1 to 10, how hesitant or scared would you be to change your course path, even with the guidance of the automatic simulator?**  
A: I would say it’s pretty scary to change my existing plan.  

**Q: On a scale of 1 to 10, what would you rate it?**  
A: Eight.  
